---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  DOWNLOAD_APK,
  DOWNLOAD_COMPANION,
  DOWNLOAD_SUMS,
  RELEASE_NOTES,
  CHANGELOG,
  VERSION,
  VERSION_SHORT,
  RELEASE_DATE,
  REPO,
} from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title={`What's new in PhonePad ${VERSION_SHORT}`}
  description={`PhonePad ${VERSION_SHORT}: play over your phone's hotspot or USB tethering, see when Windows blocks it, and pair securely. Update the app and the companion.`}
  article
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="small muted">PhonePad {VERSION} · {RELEASE_DATE}</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem)">What's new in {VERSION_SHORT}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        No Wi-Fi router? Play over the phone's own hotspot or USB tethering.
        The PC now tells you when Windows is in the way, and pairing is built so
        nothing on your network can break it.
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin-block:2rem">
        <h3>Update both the app and the companion</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          A phone that is already paired keeps working across the update.
          Pairing a phone needs {VERSION_SHORT} on both sides, and each one
          tells you if the other is out of date.
        </p>
        <div class="cta" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DOWNLOAD_APK} rel="noopener">Download the app</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={DOWNLOAD_COMPANION} rel="noopener">Download the companion</a>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:1rem;margin-bottom:0">
          Install the new app over the old one; your layouts and pairings stay.
          Replace the old companion with the new file. Checksums are in
          <a href={DOWNLOAD_SUMS} rel="noopener">SHA256SUMS.txt</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Play over the phone's hotspot or USB tethering</h2>
      <p>
        Turn on the phone's hotspot and connect the PC to it, or plug the phone
        in and turn on USB tethering. PhonePad finds the PC over either.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before, the phone only searched the network Android uses for the
        internet, and when the phone is sharing its connection that is mobile
        data, so the PC was never found
        (<a href={`${REPO}/issues/2`} rel="noopener">issue #2</a>). It now
        searches every local network it is part of, including the ones it is
        providing, and leaves mobile data out so the search never goes to your
        carrier.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is free, like everything that makes the controller reach the PC.
        <a href="/setup/">The setup guide</a> has the steps.
      </p>

      <h2>The companion tells you when Windows is blocking PhonePad</h2>
      <p>
        Windows treats any network it has not seen before as public, and blocks
        phones from reaching PhonePad there. That was the other half of
        "No PC found" on a hotspot, and nothing said so.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now the companion shows <em>Windows is blocking PhonePad</em>, names the
        network, and says why. For your own phone's hotspot or tethering it
        offers <strong>Make private</strong>: one administrator prompt, and only
        that network is trusted. A Wi-Fi hotspot is remembered after that. With
        USB tethering, many phones present a new network each time tethering
        starts, so Windows may ask again.
      </p>
      <p>
        PhonePad does not open its ports on public networks in general. That
        was suggested as a fix, and it would have included every café, hotel and
        airport network the PC ever joins.
      </p>

      <h2>Pairing that cannot be broken offline</h2>
      <p>
        In 1.0, a device on the same network could recover the six-digit code
        from the PC's first pairing reply while the pairing window was open, and
        pair itself as a trusted phone. In {VERSION_SHORT} each side commits to
        its proof of the code before the other reveals one, and the PC shows a
        new code after every attempt, so a device without the code gets one
        guess per code, and pairing stops after five.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you ever paired while running 1.0 on a network you do not control,
        look at <em>Trusted phones</em> in the companion and forget anything you
        do not recognise. The details are in
        <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`} rel="noopener">SECURITY.md</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Ads, kept out of the way</h2>
      <p>
        PhonePad stays free. The app's home, layouts and settings screens have
        one ad strip at the bottom: never on the controller, never over a
        button, and not at all where there is no room for it, such as a phone
        held sideways. The companion has one sponsor strip. Where the law asks,
        Google's consent message comes first, and your choice can be changed in
        <em>Settings → Ads</em>. What each strip sends is in the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Smaller things</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          If USB tethering is switched off and on mid-game, the phone reconnects
          by itself instead of waiting on "reconnecting" until restarted.
        </li>
        <li>
          The phone lists each network it is on by what it is: Wi-Fi, Hotspot,
          USB tethering. It used to call a mobile-data address "Wi-Fi address".
        </li>
        <li>
          The companion shows its version, which networks the PC is on, and how
          many search probes it has heard from phones, which tells a network
          problem apart from everything else.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Not in this release</h2>
      <p>
        Several phones as separate controllers, for local multiplayer, is
        planned for 1.2. It fits the design, and it touches the part that
        guarantees a dropped phone can never leave a button held, so it gets a
        release of its own.
        <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/ADR-002-multiplayer.md`} rel="noopener">The plan is public.</a>
      </p>

      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:3rem">
        Every change is in the <a href={CHANGELOG} rel="noopener">changelog</a>
        and on the <a href={RELEASE_NOTES} rel="noopener">release page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
